---
import DocLink from '@/components/DocLink.astro';
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="troubleshooting"
  title="Motion: troubleshooting"
  description="What to check when a motion of @udixio/motion does not play, is ignored on a component, erases a style, stretches text, loses focus, keeps running, takes the spacing away, replays on hydration or ignores reduced motion in a shadow root: each symptom with its cause and its fix."
>
  <p>
    Find your symptom, then check its causes in order. Each cause says what the runtime does, and what to change. To see what
    the runtime holds on a page, call <code>inspect()</code> from <code>@udixio/motion/scope</code>: it lists the
    reduced-motion policy and its source, each binding with its element, kind, name and state, and the defined motions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="nothing-animates">Nothing animates</h2>

  <h3>The content was there before the runtime</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> An entry plays on a change that happens after the element is bound. Content that was on the page
    first is already in place, so it plays no entry:
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">An element that is on the page when <code>connectMotion()</code> runs is already in place, and a reveal that is in view when it is bound stays as it is.</p>
    <p slot="react">React treats hydrated server content as already in place.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Angular marks the elements it renders on the server <code>data-motion-presence="present"</code>, which binds them without an entry.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Svelte plays the entry of server content again when it hydrates: see the hydration symptom below.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Make the change after binding: insert the element, or make its state change, such as its
    <code>present</code>, <code>&#64;if</code> or <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code> turning true; in HTML, remove its
    <code>hidden</code> attribute. To play an entry as the page opens, bind the element yourself with
    <code>motion(element, 'slide')</code> from <code>@udixio/motion</code>: it plays the entry of an element that carries no
    <code>data-motion</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>A class hides the element</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> The runtime follows the <code>hidden</code> attribute in HTML, and the state of each adapter, such
    as <code>present</code> or a condition. It does not read your classes. A class that sets <code>display: none</code>, such
    as Tailwind's <code>hidden</code> utility, hides the element at once, before any exit; an element that a class keeps at
    <code>display: none</code> plays its entry where nobody can see it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Toggle the state, not a class: <code>element.hidden = true</code> in HTML, or the condition or
    <code>present</code> in a framework. During the exit the runtime keeps the element displayed, then lets
    <code>hidden</code> take over. Check that no rule of yours gives the element a <code>display</code> that beats
    <code>[hidden]</code>; Tailwind's base styles already make <code>[hidden]</code> win.
  </p>

  <h3>Motion is turned off or reduced</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the element or an ancestor (the Tailwind utility
    <code>motion-none</code>) applies the end state at once. Under reduced motion, from the system setting or from
    <code>&lt;html data-motion-policy="reduce"&gt;</code>, an entry and an exit become a short fade, an emphasis applies its
    end frame, a reveal shows at once, a layout takes its new place at once and a loop does not play.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Read <code>getComputedStyle(element).getPropertyValue('--udx-motion')</code>, and
    <code>motionPolicy.reduced</code> from <code>@udixio/motion/policy</code>; <code>inspect()</code> names the source of the
    policy. Test with reduced motion on too: it is a setting of your visitors, not a defect. See
    <DocLink href="/motion/accessibility">Accessibility</DocLink>. If the element sits in a shadow root, see
    <a href="#reduced-motion-in-a-shadow-root">Reduced motion inside a shadow root</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>The name is not defined yet</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> An unknown name leaves the element untouched. <code>connectMotion()</code> and the adapters bind
    the element as soon as the name is defined, but a motion bound that late is settled: it skips the entry it missed, and only
    the next change plays.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Call <code>defineMotion</code> in a module that runs before the elements that use it render, such as
    your app's main module, not in a component that renders later.
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">In development, <code>connectMotion(&#123; development: true &#125;)</code> logs an error that names the missing motion and lists the defined ones, and the element waits for it.</p>
    <p slot="react">In development, React logs an error that names the missing motion and lists the defined ones, and the element waits for it.</p>
    <p slot="angular">In development, Angular logs an error that names the missing motion and lists the defined ones, and the element waits for it.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">In development, Svelte logs an error that names the missing motion and lists the defined ones, and the element waits for it.</p>
  </ForFramework>

  <h3>The presets are not imported</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> <code>fade</code>, <code>slide</code>, <code>scale</code>, <code>reflow</code> and the other
    presets are defined by the import path <code>@udixio/motion/presets</code>. Without it, their names are unknown, as above.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Import it once, next to <code>connectMotion()</code> or in your app's main module:
    <code>import '@udixio/motion/presets';</code>. See <DocLink href="/motion/presets">Presets</DocLink>.
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Without a bundler, map <code>@udixio/motion/presets</code> in your import map too.</p>
  </ForFramework>

  <h2 id="ignored-on-component">Your motion is ignored on a component</h2>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">This symptom is about the Udixio components, which are React, Angular and Svelte components. In HTML, <code>data-motion</code> goes on your own element, and nothing sits between it and its box.</p>
    <p slot="react">A motion on a Udixio component, through <code>&lt;Motion as=&#123;Component&#125;&gt;</code>, moves the component's <DocLink href="/motion/glossary#term-box">box</DocLink>, its root element.</p>
    <p slot="angular">A motion on a Udixio component, through <code>udxMotion</code> on its tag, moves the component's <DocLink href="/motion/glossary#term-box">box</DocLink>, its root element.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">A motion on a Udixio component, through <code>&#123;&#64;attach motion()&#125;</code>, moves the component's <DocLink href="/motion/glossary#term-box">box</DocLink>, its root element.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    When nothing moves, development says why on the console, once per element, if the
    <a href="#development-checks">development checks</a> are on.
  </p>

  <h3>The component already holds that kind</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> A component that animates one of its elements holds that
    <DocLink href="/motion/glossary#term-reserved-kind">kind</DocLink> there: a Snackbar holds the presence of its panel, a SideSheet the
    presence and the layout of its panel, a NavigationRail the layout of its rail. A motion of that kind on the same element
    is ignored, and the console names the owner:
  </p>
  <p><code>[@udixio/motion] "fade" ignored: Snackbar already holds a presence on this element, for its own animation: an element carries one motion of each kind.</code></p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Time the component's own animation with its <code>motion</code> prop, where it has one (a Snackbar, a
    SideSheet, a Tooltip, a NavigationRail). A motion of another kind still plays: a <code>press</code> on a Snackbar works.
    Each component's page says which kinds it holds, and <DocLink href="/motion/choosing-an-api#element-or-component">Choosing an API</DocLink>
    lists them all. For a SideSheet:
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="react"><code>motion=&#123;&#123; timing: 'spring.slow.spatial' &#125;&#125;</code></p>
    <p slot="angular"><code>[motion]="&#123; timing: 'spring.slow.spatial' &#125;"</code></p>
    <p slot="svelte"><code>motion=&#123;&#123; timing: 'spring.slow.spatial' &#125;&#125;</code></p>
  </ForFramework>

  <h3>The component has no box to give</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> Its surface is in a portal or the top layer, away from where you write it. Its element carries
    <code>data-motion-box="none"</code>, and the motion binds nothing rather than moving its anchor. These components do it:
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="react">a Tooltip, an AnchorPositioner, a ContextMenu and a modal SideSheet. The console names the element that <code>&lt;Motion as=&#123;Tooltip&#125;&gt;</code> renders:</p>
    <p slot="angular">an anchor positioner (<code>udx-anchor-positioner</code>), a context menu (<code>udx-context-menu</code>) and a modal side sheet (<code>udx-side-sheet</code>). The console names the host, here <code>&lt;udx-context-menu…&gt;</code> with its first classes.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">a ContextMenu. The console names its root, a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="react"><code>[@udixio/motion] "press" ignored: &lt;span&gt; has no box to give (data-motion-box="none"): its component shows its surface elsewhere (a portal, the top layer). Animate the component with its own motion prop.</code></p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Time the component with its own <code>motion</code> prop where it has one: a Tooltip
    (<code>udxTooltipMotion</code> in Angular) or a SideSheet. A ContextMenu and an AnchorPositioner have none: put the motion
    on the element you give them, such as the trigger, or on the content inside them.
  </p>

  <h3>A wrapper is display: contents from a stylesheet only</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> The runtime reads the signals of an element with no box without the computed style: its own
    <code>style="display: contents"</code>, <code>data-motion-box="child"</code>, or the <code>boxless</code> option. A
    wrapper that a class or a stylesheet makes <code>display: contents</code> is not declared, so the motion stays on it, and
    a wrapper with no box has nothing to move. The console says so:
  </p>
  <p><code>[@udixio/motion] &lt;div.toast-host&gt; has no box (display: contents): declare it so its motion goes to its box (data-motion-box="child" on it, or the boxless option).</code></p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Add <code>data-motion-box="child"</code> to the wrapper. If it has several element children, mark
    the one that moves with <code>data-motion-box</code>: otherwise its first element child moves, and the console says
    <code>has no box (display: contents) and several children: mark the box with data-motion-box</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="inline-style">Your inline style is ignored or erased</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> While a presence, an emphasis or a reveal plays, the runtime writes <code>opacity</code>,
    <code>translate</code>, <code>scale</code> and <code>rotate</code> inline on the element; it never writes
    <code>transform</code>. At rest, it removes the ones that are back at their neutral value (opacity 1, no translation,
    scale 1, no rotation), so your stylesheet applies again. A value that is not neutral stays inline, such as the opacity 0
    of an exit until <code>hidden</code> takes over. So an inline style you set on one of these properties is overwritten
    while the element is bound, and removed at rest.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Set these properties with a class (<code>opacity-80</code>, <code>scale-105</code>,
    <code>hover:scale-105</code>): a class applies at rest, between motions. Or move the style to a wrapper element that has
    no motion. A gesture writes <code>translate</code> as well, so the same holds for a dragged element.
  </p>

  <h2 id="layout-not-observed">A layout move does not play after a style change</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> A layout motion watches its scope, the closest ancestor with <code>data-motion-scope</code> or else
    the element's parent: a class or <code>data-*</code> change, a child added, removed or reordered, or <code>hidden</code>
    changing. An inline <code>style</code> change is not observed, nor is a change outside the scope. The layout engine also
    loads the first time a layout motion is bound, and a change made before it has loaded is not animated.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Change a class or a <code>data-*</code> attribute instead of the inline style. When you must write
    the style, give the element the value that changes with it as its state: each new value calls <code>play()</code>, which
    compares the layout once the style is applied. Widen the scope with <code>data-motion-scope</code> on an ancestor that
    holds the change. Call <code>await loadLayoutEngine()</code> to load the engine ahead of the first change.
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">The state is <code>data-motion-state</code>, set on the element.</p>
    <p slot="react">The state is the <code>state</code> prop of <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>.</p>
    <p slot="angular">The state is <code>[udxMotionState]</code>, bound to the value.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">The state is <code>state</code> in the options of <code>motion()</code>.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>From a control, call <code>play('layout')</code> on the binding, which does the same.</p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-troubleshooting-layout" live={false} />

  <h2 id="stretched-text">Text is stretched during a layout move</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> A layout move scales the element from its old size to its new one. The elements inside it keep
    their natural size, up to three levels deep and 24 elements, but text placed directly in the moving element has no element
    of its own to correct, so it is stretched with the box.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Wrap the text in an element. Keep <code>transform-origin</code> at the centre of the moving element,
    where the move scales it.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-troubleshooting-stretched" live={false} />

  <h2 id="presence-child">An element leaves without its exit</h2>
  <p><strong>Cause.</strong></p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Removing the element from the DOM takes it away at once, with no exit: nothing can play after it is gone.</p>
    <p slot="react">The <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> keeps a child that left rendered until every <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> inside it reports that its exit has ended. A motion run by the <code>useMotion</code> hook does not report to the Presence, so a child that holds only a hook leaves at once. A child with no motion leaves at once too, and so does every child when you stop rendering the Presence itself.</p>
    <p slot="angular">When the element that leaves is in a child component's template, Angular does not wait for its exit.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">When a parent block removes the element, Svelte plays no transition: the element has to sit in the block that adds and removes it.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p><strong>Fix.</strong></p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Set <code>hidden</code> instead of removing the element: the exit plays, then the element is hidden.</p>
    <p slot="react">Render the animated element with <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>; <code>as</code> chooses its tag. Give each child of the Presence a <code>key</code>: it is how the Presence knows which child left. Keep the Presence rendered, and put the condition inside it. Keep <code>useMotion</code> for an element outside a Presence, which its <code>mounted</code> flag keeps rendered while it leaves.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Put <code>udxMotion</code> on the child's host element, in the parent's template, so the <code>&#64;if</code> keeps the host until the exit has played.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Put <code>transition:presence</code> on the element that its own <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code> or <code>&#123;#each&#125;</code> block adds and removes.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-troubleshooting-presence-child" live={false} />

  <h2 id="element-keeps-its-place">An element that left still takes its place</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> <code>setPresent(false)</code> on a <code>motion()</code> binding plays the exit, then leaves the
    element in the first frame of its motion: transparent for the presets, and <code>inert</code>. It keeps its box in the
    layout, and the server HTML shows it. Only <code>hidden</code> takes an element out of the layout.
  </p>
  <p><strong>Fix.</strong></p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Set <code>hidden</code> on the element once <code>setPresent(false)</code> has resolved to <code>'finished'</code>.</p>
    <p slot="react">Give the element <code>present</code> on <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>: it sets <code>hidden</code> once the exit has played. With <code>useMotion</code>, also set <code>hidden=&#123;!open&#125;</code> on the element.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Bind <code>[udxMotionPresent]</code> to the element: it sets <code>hidden</code> once the exit has played.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Pass <code>present</code> in the options of <code>motion()</code>: it sets <code>hidden</code> once the exit has played. The server runs no attachment, so write <code>hidden=&#123;!open&#125;</code> on the element as well.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="react">For content that leaves the page, let the <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> remove it.</p>
    <p slot="angular">For content that leaves the page, let the <code>&#64;if</code> remove it.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">For content that leaves the page, let the <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code> remove it, with <code>transition:presence</code>.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    For content that stays in the DOM, such as an accordion's answers, see the <DocLink href="/motion/recipes/accordion">accordion recipe</DocLink>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="focus-lost">Focus is lost when an element leaves</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> An exit makes the element <code>inert</code>, and the browser then drops the focus that was inside
    it. Without a target, the runtime keeps the element focusable until focus leaves it or the exit ends, and a development
    build warns once per element.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Name where focus goes, usually the control that opened the element (WCAG 2.4.3). The runtime moves
    focus there before it sets <code>inert</code>; a target that cannot take focus gets <code>tabindex="-1"</code> for that
    move. Pass a function when the target renders after the element binds.
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Set <code>data-motion-restore-focus="#opener"</code> on the element.</p>
    <p slot="react">Pass <code>restoreFocus</code> to <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>, or to <code>useMotion</code>.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Bind <code>[udxMotionRestoreFocus]</code> to the element.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Pass <code>restoreFocus</code> in the options of <code>motion()</code>.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-presence-focus" live={false} />

  <h2 id="loop-off-screen">A loop or an autoplay keeps running off-screen</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> A <code>loop</code> motion pauses by itself while its element is out of view or its tab is hidden,
    and does not play under reduced motion. Something that keeps running is not a loop of <code>@udixio/motion</code>: a CSS
    animation, a <code>setInterval</code> that advances a carousel, an <code>animateStyle</code> started by a timer.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Define the motion with the verb <code>loop</code>, or run your own timer only while the element is
    active: <code>watchActive(element, onChange)</code> from <code>@udixio/motion/active</code> tells you when it is in view, in
    a shown tab, and with motion not reduced. An element that moves inside a box that clips it, such as a carousel track with
    <code>overflow: hidden</code>, is in view while that box is: a loop finds that box by itself, and
    <code>watchActive(viewedBox(element), onChange)</code> does the same for your timer.
  </p>
  <p>Anything that moves for more than five seconds also needs a visible pause control (WCAG 2.2.2), which holds the loop:</p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html"><code>data-motion-paused</code> on the element.</p>
    <p slot="react">the <code>paused</code> prop of <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>.</p>
    <p slot="angular"><code>[udxMotionPaused]</code> on the element.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">the <code>paused</code> option of <code>motion()</code>.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>See <DocLink href="/motion/loop">Loop</DocLink>.</p>

  <h2 id="page-jumps">The page jumps when content enters</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> An entry animates the opacity and the transform of the element, which do not move the layout. The
    element takes its full place the moment it is inserted or shown, and the content below it moves at once; when an exit
    ends, its place is freed at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Reserve the space when you know the size: a minimum height on the container, an aspect ratio on
    media. Take overlays out of the flow: a dialog, a menu or a toast over the page is <code>fixed</code> or
    <code>absolute</code>. Otherwise give the neighbours <code>reflow</code>, so that they glide to their new place; in a list,
    the exit option <code>out-of-flow</code>, with a positioned parent, lets them close the gap while the item fades. See
    <DocLink href="/motion/lists">Lists</DocLink>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="spacing-around-component">Spacing disappears around a component with a motion</h2>
  <p><strong>Cause.</strong></p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="react"><code>&lt;Motion as=&#123;Component&#125;&gt;</code> creates no box, but it puts an element of its own between your parent and the component. Utilities that style the parent's direct children, or compare siblings, then reach that element, which has no box, and not the component: <code>space-x-*</code>, <code>space-y-*</code>, <code>divide-*</code>, <code>[&amp;&gt;*]:…</code>, and <code>first:</code>, <code>last:</code> or <code>peer-*</code> on the component.</p>
    <p slot="angular">The host element of every Udixio Angular component sits between your parent and the component, motion or not. Utilities that style the parent's direct children, or compare siblings, then reach that host, which has no box, and not the component: <code>space-x-*</code>, <code>space-y-*</code>, <code>divide-*</code>, <code>[&amp;&gt;*]:…</code>, and <code>first:</code>, <code>last:</code> or <code>peer-*</code> on the component.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">A Svelte attachment adds no element, so this does not happen in Svelte.</p>
    <p slot="html">A motion in HTML sits on your own element, which adds no element, so this does not happen.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Space the components with <code>gap</code> on a flex or grid parent:
    <code>&lt;div className="flex gap-4"&gt;</code>. An element with no box takes no place in the layout, so the component's
    root is the flex or grid item, and the gap applies to it. Put a border or a margin on the component itself, through its
    <code>className</code> (<code>class</code> in Angular), rather than from the parent.
  </p>

  <h2 id="development-checks">The development checks are missing in your bundler's dev build</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> The package has two builds. The default build runs the development checks (the validation of the
    specs, and the warnings) only where <code>process.env.NODE_ENV</code> is not <code>production</code>; a browser has no
    <code>process</code>, so there they are off, as in production. The development build always runs them, and the
    <code>development</code> export condition selects it. Vite's dev server, webpack in development mode and
    <code>node --conditions=development</code> use that condition; a bundler that is not set up for it loads the default build.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Add the <code>development</code> condition to your bundler's resolve conditions for development
    builds only, for example <code>--conditions=development</code> with esbuild. The runtime messages of
    <code>connectMotion()</code>, such as an unknown name, need <code>connectMotion(&#123; development: true &#125;)</code>
    in any build. The framework adapters follow the same checks: they report the same messages wherever the checks are on.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reduced-motion-in-a-shadow-root">Reduced motion does not apply inside a shadow root</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> The stylesheet's rules start from <code>:root</code>: <code>:root[data-motion-policy='reduce']</code>
    and the media query set <code>--udx-motion-scale</code> to 0 on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, and the durations that multiply by
    it, such as the page transitions and Search's outline, take it. A selector that starts from <code>html</code>, such as
    <code>html[data-motion-policy=reduce] &amp;</code>, does not cross a shadow boundary. A custom property does: the scale
    inherits into the shadow root, so the motion there is reduced too, unless a declaration inside the shadow root sets
    <code>--udx-motion-scale</code> again. Without the document stylesheet, the attribute alone does not reduce a CSS motion
    inside a shadow root.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Load the stylesheet, <code>@udixio/motion/motion.css</code>, in the document, and leave
    <code>--udx-motion-scale</code> alone inside the shadow root. For the system setting, a media query works inside a shadow
    root with or without the stylesheet: Tailwind's <code>motion-reduce:</code> variant, or
    <code>@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)</code> in the styles of the shadow root.
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">In HTML, <code>connectMotion()</code> scans the document, not the shadow roots in it, and its <code>root</code> option takes a Document or an Element: bind the elements of a shadow root yourself with <code>motion(element, 'fade')</code>.</p>
    <p>React, Angular and Svelte bind the element that their components render, not by searching the document, so their motions bind in a shadow root as they do elsewhere.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    <strong>The exception: Search.</strong> Udixio's Search has its own behaviour, and it is not the rule of the other
    components. It respects the system preference even without the stylesheet, and its script reads
    <code>data-motion-policy</code> on the document element. Its outline transition takes
    <code>--udx-motion-duration-short4</code>, 200 ms, times <code>--udx-motion-scale</code>, on the easing
    <code>--udx-motion-easing-standard-decelerate</code>. The stylesheet provides both tokens: with it, the easing is
    <code>cubic-bezier(0, 0, 0, 1)</code>; without it, the component falls back to <code>cubic-bezier(0, 0, 0.2, 1)</code>.
    Do not generalize this to all components or providers: check the one you use.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hydration">Server-rendered content flashes or replays its entry on hydration</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> The server cannot play a motion, and an option such as <code>present</code> acts in the browser
    only. What the runtime does with server HTML depends on the framework:
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">An element that is on the page when <code>connectMotion()</code> runs is already in place, so it plays no entry.</p>
    <p slot="react">React treats hydrated content as already in place: no entry. The exits of <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> exist in the browser only, since the server renders the current children.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Angular marks the present elements it renders on the server <code>data-motion-presence="present"</code>, and the browser binds them without an entry.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Svelte cannot run an attachment on the server, so the element is not marked, and its entry replays on hydration.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> For content that starts closed, send it closed: give it the <code>hidden</code> attribute in the
    server HTML, so that it never shows before the browser binds it.
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="svelte">Pass <code>settled: true</code> in the options of <code>motion()</code> for content the server renders: <code>&#123;&#64;attach motion('slide', () =&gt; (&#123; settled: true &#125;))&#125;</code>. An element with <code>present</code> needs <code>hidden=&#123;!open&#125;</code> in its markup too, since the server runs no attachment.</p>
  </ForFramework>

  <h2 id="react-hydration-warning">React warns that the server HTML did not match</h2>
  <ForFramework>
    <div slot="react">
      <p>
        <strong>Cause.</strong> <code>connectMotion()</code> binds the <code>data-motion</code> elements as soon as it runs, which
        can be before React hydrates them: it writes <code>data-motion-presence</code>, <code>inert</code> or an inline
        <code>opacity</code> on them. React's development build compares these attributes with its first render and reports a
        hydration mismatch.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Fix.</strong> In a React tree rendered on the server, bind motions with <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> or
        <code>useMotion</code>: they bind once React has hydrated. Keep <code>data-motion</code> for markup that React does not
        render. The <DocLink href="/motion/recipes/accordion">accordion recipe</DocLink> shows an answer kept in the DOM with
        <code>&lt;Motion present&gt;</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>This warning is React's. For what the server content does in the other frameworks, see the hydration symptom above.</p>
  </ForFramework>
</MotionPage>
